## Objective
Learn how TCP Window Size controls the amount of data that can be transmitted before receiving an acknowledgment (ACK) and 
understand its importance in network performance analysis.

## What I Learned

- TCP Window Size controls how much data can be sent before an acknowledgment is received.
- A larger Window Size generally improves data transfer performance.
- A very small Window Size can slow down communication because fewer bytes are transmitted before waiting for an ACK.
- Wireshark displays the Window, Calculated Window Size, and Window Size Scaling Factor within the TCP header.
- Security analysts analyze Window Size to troubleshoot network performance and identify communication bottlenecks.

